A||||8||mwbackus||Wing repair||||1
Z||000000||mwbackus||10-31-1999||09:29 AM||||I have a rear spoiler (wing) that has some problems.  The bolt inserts (that the stantons mount to) in the wing itself have rusted, one was so bad that it disintegrated upon stanton removal.  It was not fun removing the pieces that ended up inside the wing.  My questions concern the possible repair of this wing.  I have no idea what is available to replace these "bolt inserts" or if something even exists.  I have given thought to the possible use of toggle bolts, but would prefer a more professional fix.<BR>I am sure that this is a subject that will be of concern to many, as our little cars age and the elements take their toll.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000001||kornholio||10-31-1999||10:04 AM||||I think the bolt inserts you are talking about are available from FIERODAVE's website.<BR>Check them out at <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5845/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5845/index.html</A>  .||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||10-31-1999||07:23 PM||||No, wrong end of the stantion. :-(  These inserts are what is left IN the wing if you completely remove the stantions from it.  It seems that the Pontiac engineers had a severe case of brain cloud when they thought these little buggers up.  They are not made of stainless, more like soft steel that will  eventually rust in every Fiero currently on the road.  This wing was from an 85 GT, with less than 90k miles.  The only way to discover this little problem is to completly remove the stantions from the wing.  But, given time and enough corrosion, they will probably disassemble themselves.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||superXmotorsports||10-31-1999||09:44 PM||||Your local body shop should be able to replace those rusted pieces with new mounts and screws to hold the stand to the wing.  I had a rusty one a year or so ago and that is what I had to do.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000004||mwbackus||11-01-1999||06:21 AM||||How much would a bodyshop charge for something like this?  I doubt many could justify much over $50 to repair a $200 wing.  The reason I am researching this is to determine where to obtain the correct parts needed for the repair, and how one may do this repair themselves for a reasonable expense.  This kind of information is what this forum is about, and this is something that most of us will encounter in the not too distant future.  Perhaps there are body shop experts here on the forum who could offer some assistance?||||reg||1
Z||000005||dogtired||11-01-1999||07:34 AM||||Been There Done That!<BR>So you can't attach your stantions to the wing? What I did was, in the area where the stantions attach to the wing there is a grove, I cut with a drill a hole about 1/4 by 1 1/4 inch (when the stantion is attached it will cover that hole) through which I was able to remove the old parts of the fasteners, using a magnet, and then I was able to insert a Nut-Plate (a piece of metal with nuts brazed in the correct location. I also covered the nut-plates with RTV so when I screwed the new screws through the stantions it would glue them in place. The biggest problem I had was finding the proper length (you don't want to screw it out through the top of your wing) and head screw to use because the heads show on the back-under side. The ones I ended up with are a bit bigger (heads)  than stock, but a lot better than having no wing at all? There are probably other ways and body shops would probably have a fit with this method, but my wing is as strong as any and the only thing you can see are the larger screw heads.<BR>Hope this helps. I tried some wall anchors, the type that spread out when you put in the screw but they were not successful at all.<BR>The material the wing is constructed of is about 1/4 in thick. Good luck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000006||mwbackus||11-01-1999||08:19 AM||||     Thanks Dogtired, very useful input!  <BR>My 85GT wing is not in very good condition overall, it has been kicking around the garage for some time now, but does provides an excellent "test subject" for an evaluation of different ways to address this problem.<BR>     I am sure that these ideas will be of benefit to all those with spoiler equiped Fiero's in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000007||superXmotorsports||11-01-1999||06:06 PM||||I paid $30 to have that repair made.||||reg||4
